<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tlapanec people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Me'phaa, Tlapanecs</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tlapanecs, who call themselves Me'phaa, are an indigenous people of the mountains of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrero">Guerrero</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, speaking an Oto-Manguean language. In pre-Hispanic times the Yopes of Yopitzinco, a Tlapanec lordship near the Pacific coast, resisted conquest by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a>. Today more than 100,000 people speak the Me'phaa language.</p></div>
